RT64 port patch 0007: present scheduling for high refresh rates and VRR, and
pivots for the frame interpolation (Blast Corps port; MIT, as RT64).

Pivots: RigidBody::lerp interpolates a transform's decomposition, with the
model origin's world position moved along a straight line.  A part turning
about an axle away from its model origin (the game's vehicle wheels) swings
that origin round the axle, so in the in-between pictures the part left its
axle.  TransformGroup::pivot (set by the host after matrixId,
live_interp.cpp) names a point in the model space the transform turns
about: both matrices are decomposed with their origin moved to it, the
lerp's result moved back (RigidBody::lerpDecomposed is lerp's former body).
At the end weights the result is the matrix itself; without a pivot nothing
changes.

RT64 paces the pictures between the game's with a timer that sleeps 1 / target s
from the last present, and plume's D3D12 swap chain presents with a sync
interval of 0 (present wait).  At a target equal to the display's rate with
vsync on, the timer beats against the refreshes: a picture replaced before it
was shown, the one before held two refreshes (the "8 / 25 ms" alternation); a
late wake-up also delays every picture after it.  And the workload queue
renders one in-between picture fewer (frameReduction) whenever it had to wait
for the present thread at all.

PresentQueue::scheduling (the host sets it; Legacy keeps everything as before):
  - Locked: with vsync on (D3D12) and a target rate that divides the display's,
    each picture is presented with a sync interval of display / target and no
    timer: the frame-latency wait (latency 1) lets one picture queue, so the
    display shows one per refresh (per N refreshes);
  - otherwise (and always with Vrr) an even timer: each due time one period
    after the last due time (not after the last present call, so a late
    wake-up or a wait in the swap chain doesn't delay every picture after
    it); a picture not ready by its due time is presented when it is and the
    timer goes on from there (no burst to catch up);
  - Vrr: the even timer with a sync interval of 1 when vsync is on (a variable
    refresh display, G-Sync / FreeSync, shows each picture when it comes; the
    host caps the target a little under the display's maximum), tearing
    allowed when vsync is off (plume's own present).
The vsync pacing of patch 0005 still comes first when it is on.  With any
scheduling but Legacy, frameReduction counts only waits over half a picture
(as 0005 does for its pacing).  For the host's frame-time report:
PresentedInfo gets the target and original rates, the sync interval used and
when the picture was drawn; counters of skipped presents and timer restarts
(PresentQueue) and of in-between pictures reduced, skipped late or cut short
and of all pictures rendered (WorkloadQueue).

